    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Union of two half-discs",
        "body": "The two functions are the upper unit half-circles centered at $(1,0)$ and $(2,0)$ (each defined where the root is real: $[0,2]$ and $[1,3]$). The integral is the area of the union of the two half-discs: $\\frac\\pi2+\\frac\\pi2-(\\text{overlap})$.\n\nThe two unit circles have centers at distance $1$, so the overlap of the full discs is a lens of area $2\\cdot\\frac\\pi3-\\frac{\\sqrt3}2$ (formula $2r^2\\cos^{-1}\\frac d{2r}-\\frac d2\\sqrt{4r^2-d^2}$). The lens is symmetric about the $x$-axis, so the overlap of the upper half-discs is $\\frac\\pi3-\\frac{\\sqrt3}4$. Hence\n$$\\text{union}=\\frac\\pi2+\\frac\\pi2-\\left(\\frac\\pi3-\\frac{\\sqrt3}4\\right)=\\frac{2\\pi}3+\\frac{\\sqrt3}4\\approx2.5274.$$\nCheck by direct integration: the curves cross at $x=\\frac32$ and the integral equals $2\\int_0^{3/2}\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}dx=2\\left(\\frac\\pi4+\\frac\\pi{12}+\\frac{\\sqrt3}8\\right)$, the same value (also confirmed numerically).\n\n**Answer: $\\frac{2\\pi}3+\\frac{\\sqrt3}4$**"
      }
